Phase 1: Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

Before touching any model, you need to understand your data. Load it, check the shape, data types, missing values, and distributions. Look at correlations between features and your target variables.

Why we do this in simple terms: Think of it like inspecting ingredients before cooking. You wouldn’t start a recipe without knowing what’s in your fridge, what’s expired, and what tastes good together. EDA tells you what data you’re working with, what’s missing, and what relationships already exist before you commit to a modeling approach.

Phase 2: Feature Selection and Engineering

Start broad, then narrow down using correlation analysis, multicollinearity checks, and feature importance from a baseline model. Engineer new features like ratios and time-based aggregates.

Why we do this in simple terms: Models can only learn from what you give them. Feature selection is about removing noise so the model focuses on signals that matter. Feature engineering is about creating smarter inputs from raw data — for example, instead of just “balance” and “payment,” you create “payment-to-balance ratio,” which tells a richer story about customer behavior.

Phase 3: Unsupervised Exploration with KMeans

Run KMeans clustering. Use the elbow method and silhouette score to find the optimal number of clusters.

Why we do this in simple terms: KMeans is like sorting a pile of laundry into groups without anyone telling you the categories. The algorithm looks at all the customer data and naturally groups similar customers together based on patterns. We use it when we don’t already know the natural segments in our data. The elbow method and silhouette score are just tools to help us decide the right number of groups — too few and you lose nuance, too many and the groups become meaningless.

Phase 4: Supervised Modeling Progression

Start with decision tree, then random forest, then XGBoost. Evaluate at each step.

Why we do this in simple terms:

A decision tree is a flowchart of yes/no questions that ends in a prediction. We start here because it’s easy to understand and gives us a baseline to beat.

A random forest builds hundreds of decision trees on different slices of the data and averages their answers. We move to this because one tree can be unstable and biased — a hundred trees voting together gives a more reliable answer.

XGBoost also builds many trees, but each new tree learns from the mistakes of the previous one. We use this when we need the highest accuracy possible because that sequential learning makes it the industry standard for prediction tasks.

We progress through them in order because it shows we made the right choice at each step, and we have a clear reason for moving up the complexity ladder.

Phase 5: Evaluation and Tuning

Compare models using accuracy, ROC-AUC, precision, recall, and F1. Use stratified k-fold cross-validation. Tune hyperparameters with grid search.

Why we do this in simple terms:

Accuracy alone can be misleading, especially if one outcome is way more common than the other. So we use multiple metrics to get a full picture. ROC-AUC tells us how well the model separates the two groups. Recall tells us how many of the customers we should be catching are we actually catching. Precision tells us when the model says yes, how often is it right.

Cross-validation means splitting the data multiple times instead of just once. It’s like getting a second, third, and fourth opinion on how the model performs, which makes us much more confident in the results.

Hyperparameter tuning is adjusting the model’s settings to find the best configuration. Think of it like tuning a guitar — the same instrument sounds completely different depending on how it’s set up.

Phase 6: Documentation

Clean README explaining the business problem, approach, results, and how to run the code.

Why we do this in simple terms: Code that no one understands is useless. Documentation makes your work reproducible, shareable, and credible. For a GitHub portfolio, this is what hiring managers actually read.

